WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:03.830
This road might not look like anything special.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:09.809
But what if I showed you the same road, a
few years earlier —notice anything different?

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:17.110
Here’s a better angle of that:
The old road has 4 lanes for traffic.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.920
The new one has two.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.300
And now there’s this middle lane for left
hand turns.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.590
There’s also a new bike lane.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.240
This is what transportation planners call
a road diet.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.180
And it’s a very popular to make the roads
safer.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.860
Over the course of the 20th century, four
lane roads became an American institution.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:42.440
It started with the release of Ford’s model-T
in 1908.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:47.460
A few decades later, there was one car for
every two households in the states.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.980
By the 1960s, many roads became so busy, that
traffic engineers had to figure out how to

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.100
add capacity.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.860
So, they added lanes.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.640
A lot of pavement was getting put on the ground.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:08.210
Even where maybe when the population and traffic
volumes weren’t so high that we needed that

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.250
pavement.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.010
But we wanted that pavement.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.420
Sometimes your eyes are bigger than your stomach.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.780
Fast forward to the present day and we’re
left some overbuilt roads that are pretty

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.050
unsafe.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:26.190
And crash reduction is a major benefit that
planners can achieve with just a bit a paint.

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:30.560
Four lane roads have quite a few conflict
points -- these are places where accidents

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:31.560
could happen.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.560
These ones represent merging accidents.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.350
These are rear ends, and there are multiple
left turn crash scenarios.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:43.460
Now look at what a 3 lane reconfiguration
does — There are far fewer crash points.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.730
A road diet can also make left turns suck
way less.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:51.729
The shared middle lane takes left turners
out of the traffic flow, so they won’t hold

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:53.680
up drivers who want to continue through.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.450
And now, left turners will only have to cross
1 lane of traffic instead of 2, which will

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.590
eliminate broadside accidents.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:03.660
And the benefits don't end there.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:08.429
By slimming each lane in the road, the road
diet reduced the travel speed by almost 7

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:09.670
miles per hour.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:22.110
Narrower lanes can cause that psychological
impact on the driver to slow down a bit.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.370
And while a 6 mile an hour difference in speed
may seem modest, it can make an auto accident

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:31.310
much less deadly ...
Narrower lanes also leave more space for expanded

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:33.450
sidewalks or bike lanes.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.840
your pedestrians will feel safer.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:39.110
It might’ve given you more green space to
separate from your vehicles.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:45.380
And your bicyclists might have a dedicated
space to ride.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:50.580
In the midst of these changes, the number
of traffic lanes has gone from 2 to 1.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.959
So if you drive a car, you might assume that
the tradeoff of a road diet would be congestion...

00:02:55.959 --> 00:02:58.500
How could traffic *not* increase?

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.520
That’s usually their concern before a road
diet is implemented.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.650
That’s not what happens.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.180
The volume of the roadway is still sustained.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:12.989
We wouldn’t want to put a 4 to 3 conversion
on a piece of roadway that would then push

00:03:12.989 --> 00:03:14.850
half your traffic somewhere else.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:23.209
And what we found in a couple of places, it
actually makes moving through town easier.

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:27.550
But traffic flow is only one part of the equation
— you’ve got to also balance commercial

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.010
and safety benefits too.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:33.750
especially if this is through an urban corridor
where you’ve got businesses, coffeeshops,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:34.750
it works.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:41.930
it keeps people moving, it doesn’t take
traffic away from that corridor, and it reduces

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.150
those rear-ends that thendo stop the road.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.200
Iowa is conducting this road survey because
so far, the plan has worked *really* well

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.200
for them.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.069
A study of 15 streets in the state saw a crash
reduction rate of nearly 50%.

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:58.870
At the same time, the diet didn’t substantially
disrupt other activities along their corridors.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:05.340
A key factor was the traffic volume, measured
by engineers as ‘Annual Average Daily Traffic’.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.370
Most road diets will run into problems as
you approach that 15,000 vehicles number.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:13.030
In Iowa, many roads don’t get that much
traffic.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:16.750
The same could not be said for the suburban
contexts in California.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:22.430
A study comparing road diets in Iowa to corridors
in California showed that California’s streets

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:25.379
average about double the amount of daily traffic.

00:04:25.379 --> 00:04:31.050
The same kinds of road diets resulted in a
17% crash rate reduction -- significantly

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.539
lower than the reductions in Iowa.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.770
This is not to say that the road diets in
California are an outright failure.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:39.800
It’s just a difference *context* for the
road diet.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.659
And the success of a road diet is driven by
so many other factors -- economic impact,

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:46.990
land use, or level of service to name a few.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:48.569
What works in Iowa may not be the right fit
for California, and vice versa.

00:04:48.569 --> 00:04:53.279
So the case for road diets is pretty clear:
they do slow streets down and they do reduce

00:04:53.279 --> 00:04:54.279
crashes.

00:04:54.279 --> 00:04:58.199
But whether or not that’s worth the trouble
depends very much on the context of the world

00:04:58.199 --> 00:04:59.029
that surrounds the road.

